WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.420
PRESENTER: Welcome to Cloud
Security Basics, a series where

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:06.810
we explain the ins and outs
of securing your application

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:08.490
on Google Cloud.

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:09.570
Sound fun?

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:11.950
Then stick around,
because in this episode,

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:14.040
you'll learn about
why you shouldn't

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:17.280
log into a compromised server
instance during a breach.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:20.724
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:36.000
[PHONE BUTTONS DIALING]

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.880
BRAD ACTOR: Greetings,
cloud detective.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.670
Last episode, you used Google
Cloud's Operations suite

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:44.670
to track down a
compromised employee.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:48.090
But this time, I have finally
gotten into your system.

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:49.950
I really have.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:53.880
I repeat, I have
breached your system.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.650
So riddle me this.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:59.850
What can you do about it
now that I'm on the inside?

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:02.160
Hurry up as fast as you can.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.790
Try to get me out before I
swap all of the employee photos

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:07.908
with cat photos.

00:01:07.908 --> 00:01:08.700
FEMALE SPEAKER: OK.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.740
So here's what we know.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:14.190
Brad Actor says he's
breached my system, which

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:17.200
is a strange thing
for an attacker to do.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.200
And I haven't
received any alerts

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.030
from my monitoring system.

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:25.610
Maybe I should log in
and see what I can find.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:27.200
Actually, no.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.460
That could be playing into
exactly what Brad Actor wants.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:34.190
I almost just made
a really big mistake

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:36.980
and logged into the
compromised instance.

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:40.550
But many attackers plan
for this exact response

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:44.150
and actually try to steal admin
credentials as they log in

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:45.900
to fix the breach.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.740
So a general rule here
is to avoid logging

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:52.910
into a compromised
server during a breach.

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:53.470
Hmm.

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:56.590
So how can I see if
Brad Actor really

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:00.760
did breach my system without
compromising it further?

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.730
Well, luckily, we've
set up an external store

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:05.980
for our security logs
that we can still

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:07.990
check out what happened.

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:11.920
By putting our forensic
artifacts in a separate place--

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.290
in this case, a
Google Cloud project--

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:18.520
we can examine the problem
without alerting the attacker.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:21.640
This lets us better
understand what's happening.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:24.970
And we can watch and record
their actions to tip off

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:27.420
local authorities if needed.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:31.300
We set this up by using Google
Cloud Operation Suite's Export

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.670
feature in combination
with sinks,

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:37.330
which are basically filtered
exports for log entries.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:39.640
The supported
destinations for sinks

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:44.320
can be a cloud storage bucket,
a Pub/Sub topic, a BigQuery

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:46.870
table, or a Cloud
Logging bucket.

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:50.950
So we created a sink in our
application program, the one

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:53.440
Brad Actor says he compromised.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:56.680
And we copied those logs
into a Cloud Logging bucket

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:59.020
in our logs monitoring project.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:01.480
We ended up using a
Cloud Logging bucket

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:05.560
because unlike logs data
that's stored in Cloud Storage,

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:09.460
logs stored in Cloud Logging
are indexed, optimized,

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:12.670
and delivered to let you
analyze your logs in close

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:14.050
to real time.

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:16.240
Another benefit of
this approach is

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:18.550
that it allows us to
set up a system where

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:22.300
we view all of our
project logs in one place,

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:25.030
simplifying monitoring
everything in our cloud

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:26.440
environment.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:31.690
Looking through my logs, I don't
see any suspicious activity.

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:33.430
Hmm.

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:36.700
I guess it's best to be patient
and wait this one out and see

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:39.460
if anything does pop up.

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:43.180
Uh-oh, I got it.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:45.370
Now, that's just devious.

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:46.990
BRAD ACTOR: Drats, detective.

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:49.270
You foiled my plan.

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:50.550
It's true.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:52.900
I didn't fully
breach your system.

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:55.300
But I was hoping to
steal your credentials

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:58.090
when you attempted to log in.

00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:00.250
It would have been a
mistake to immediately log

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:03.650
into the supposedly
compromised server.

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:06.640
But I know you would have
examined your audit logs, which

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:09.460
means you must have set up a
separate security environment

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:11.110
for your forensic files.

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:12.760
Well played, detective.

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:14.530
Well played.

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:16.329
That's all from me for now.

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:19.779
But I will come back more
devious and mischievous

00:04:19.779 --> 00:04:22.040
than ever in the near future.

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:24.220
PRESENTER: So there you
have it-- another episode

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:26.440
of Cloud Security Basics.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:28.810
For more information
about securing your cloud

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:30.460
environment, be
sure to check out

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:32.930
the links in the
description below.

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:35.740
So stay tuned for the rest
of the Cloud Security Basic

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:38.470
series, because when
it comes to security,

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:40.360
you can't let bad actors win.

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:43.710
[MUSIC PLAYING]

